            "term": "AX or AXE",
            "definition": "The instruments so designated are so discriminated in Scriptures as to show that the Hebrews had them of several different forms and for various uses. The Hebrew word rendered “axe head\" in II Ki. 6:5 means literally iron; but, as an axe is certainly intended, the passage is valuable as showing that at least some of the axe-heads among the Hebrews were of iron. Those which have been found in Egypt are of bronze, which was very anciently and generally used for the purpose. The “battle axe\" (Jer. 51:20) was probably a heavy mace^ or maul. By axes, which were a part of the insignia of the Roman magistracy, was denoted the power of life and death and of supreme judgment. Eze. 26:9; Jer. 10:3; 46:22—Mat. 3:10. AXLETREES.—See CHARIOT. I Ki. 7:32, 33."
